// Chapter X: The Lobster-Quadrille — Complete (Pages 147–161)
// Includes Lobster-Quadrille Dance (#35 p. 150), "Will you walk a little faster?" (pp. 151–152),
// Lobster at Dressing-Table wrap + verse (#36 p. 157), and "Beautiful Soup" (pp. 160–161).

\use "components/Align.dt"
\use "components/Spacer.dt"
\use "components/Italic.dt"
\use "AliceStyle.dt"

\Component ChapterTen(
    x: Distance = parent.left,
    y: Distance = parent.top,
    width: Distance = parent.width,
    pagination: Pagination = \Pagination(breakable: true),
    env font: Font,
    env chapter_meta: PageValue,
    env chapter_end_meta: PageValue
) {
    \let ch = \AliceChapterPlain(
        x: x,
        y: y,
        width: width,
        number: "X",
        title: "THE LOBSTER QUADRILLE",
        verso_header: "THE LOBSTER",
        recto_header: "QUADRILLE.",
        full_header: "THE LOBSTER-QUADRILLE.",
        header_lines: 10.0,
        break_before: Break.Page
    ) {
        // ============================================================
        // Pages 147–149: Explaining the Lobster-Quadrille
        // ============================================================
        \Para {
            T\SmallCaps{HE} Mock Turtle sighed deeply, and drew the back of one
            flapper across his eyes. He looked at Alice and tried to speak, but
            for a minute or two sobs choked his voice. “Same as if he had a bone
            in his throat,” said the Gryphon, and it set to work shaking him and
            punching him in the back. At last the Mock Turtle recovered his
            voice, and, with tears running down his cheeks, he went on again:—
        }

        \Para {
            “You may not have lived much under the sea—” (“I haven’t,” said
            Alice)—“and perhaps you were never even introduced to a lobster—”
            (Alice began to say “I once tasted—” but checked herself hastily,
            and said, “No, never”)—“so you can have no idea what a delightful
            thing a Lobster\=Quadrille is!”
        }

        \Para {
            “No, indeed,” said Alice. “What sort of a dance is it?”
        }

        \Para {
            “Why,” said the Gryphon, “you first form into a line along the
            sea\=shore—”
        }

        \Para {
            “Two lines!” cried the Mock Turtle. “Seals, turtles, salmon, and
            so on: then, when you’ve cleared all the jelly\=fish out of the way—”
        }

        \Para {
            “\Italic{That} generally takes some time,” inter\-rupted the Gryphon.
        }

        \Para {
            “—you advance twice—”
        }

        \Para {
            “Each with a lobster as a partner!” cried the Gryphon.
        }

        \Para {
            “Of course,” the Mock Turtle said: “advance twice, set
            to partners—”
        }

        \Para {
            “—change lobsters, and retire in same order,” continued the
            Gryphon.
        }

        \Para {
            “Then, you know,” the Mock Turtle went on, “you throw the—”
        }

        \Para {
            “The lobsters!” shouted the Gryphon, with a bound into the air.
        }

        \Para {
            “—as far out to sea as you can—”
        }

        \Para {
            “Swim after them!” screamed the Gryphon.
        }

        \Para {
            “Turn a somersault in the sea!” cried the Mock Turtle, capering
            wildly about.
        }

        \Para {
            “Change lobsters again!” yelled the Gryphon at the top of its voice.
        }

        \Para {
            “Back to land again, and—that’s all the first figure,” said the Mock
            Turtle, suddenly dropping his voice, and the two creatures, who had
            been jumping about like mad things all this time, sat down again
            very sadly and quietly, and looked at Alice.
        }

        \Para {
            “It must be a very pretty dance,” said Alice timidly.
        }

        \Para {
            “Would you like to see a little of it?” said the Mock Turtle.
        }

        \Para {
            “Very much indeed,” said Alice.
        }

        \Para {
            “Come, let’s try the first figure!” said the Mock Turtle to the
            Gryphon. “We can do it without lobsters, you know. Which shall sing?”
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Page 150 (Verso): Top Plate #35 (Lobster-Quadrille Dance) + 7 lines
        // ============================================================
        \TopPlate(src: "images/tenniel_35.png", grid_lines: 15.0, img_top: 4pt)

        \Para {
            “Oh, \Italic{you} sing,” said the Gryphon. “I’ve forgotten the
            words.”
        }

        \Para {
            So they began solemnly dancing round and round Alice, every now and
            then treading on her toes when they passed too close, and waving their
            fore\=paws to mark the time, while the Mock Turtle sang this,
            very slowly and sadly:—
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Page 151 (Recto): "Will you walk a little faster?" (Stanzas 1 & 2)
        // ============================================================
        \PoemBlock(size: 10pt, leading: 16.4pt, italic: true, break_before: Break.Page) {
            \Spacer(height: 3pt)
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { “Will you walk a little faster?” said a whiting to a snail, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { “There’s a porpoise close behind us, and he’s treading on }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 23.8pt) { my tail. }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { See how eagerly the lobsters and the turtles all advance! }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { They are waiting on the shingle—will you come and join }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 23.8pt) { the dance? }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 10pt) { Will you, won’t you, will you, won’t you, will you join }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 23.8pt) { the dance? }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 10pt) { Will you, won’t you, will you, won’t you, won’t you join }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 23.8pt) { the dance? }

            \Spacer(height: 34pt)

            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { “You can really have no notion how delightful it will be }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { When they take us up and throw us, with the lobsters, out }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 23.8pt) { to sea!” }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { But the snail replied “Too far, too far!” and gave a look }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 23.8pt) { askance— }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { Said he thanked the whiting kindly, but he would not join }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 23.8pt) { the dance. }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 10pt) { Would not, could not, would not, could not, would not }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 23.8pt) { join the dance. }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 10pt) { Would not, could not, would not, could not, could not }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 23.8pt) { join the dance. }
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Page 152 (Verso): Stanza 3 + Whiting & Porpoise dialogue (pp. 152–156)
        // ============================================================
        \PoemBlock(size: 10pt, leading: 16.4pt, italic: true, break_before: Break.Page) {
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { “What matters it how far we go?” his scaly friend replied, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { “There is another shore, you know, upon the other side. }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { The further off from England the nearer is to France— }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 0pt) { Then turn not pale, beloved snail, but come and join the dance. }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 10pt) { Will you, won’t you, will you, won’t you, will you join }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 23.8pt) { the dance? }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 10pt) { Will you, won’t you, will you, won’t you, won’t you join }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 0pt, indent: 23.8pt) { the dance?” }
            \Spacer(height: 18pt)
        }

        \Para {
            “Thank you, it’s a very interesting dance to watch,” said Alice,
            feeling very glad that it was over at last; “and I do so like that
            curious song about the whiting!”
        }

        \Para {
            “Oh, as to the whiting,” said the Mock Turtle, “they—you’ve seen
            them, of course?”
        }

        \Para {
            “Yes,” said Alice, “I’ve often seen them at dinn—” she checked
            herself hastily.
        }

        \Para {
            “I don’t know where Dinn may be,” said the Mock Turtle, “but if
            you’ve seen them so often, of course you know what they’re like.”
        }

        \Para {
            “I believe so,” Alice replied thoughtfully. “They have their tails
            in their mouths;—and they’re all over crumbs.”
        }

        \Para {
            “You’re wrong about the crumbs,” said the Mock Turtle: “crumbs would
            all wash off in the sea. But they \Italic{have} their tails in their
            mouths; and the reason is—” here the Mock Turtle yawned and shut
            his eyes.—“Tell her about the reason and all that,” he said to the
            Gryphon.
        }

        \Para {
            “The reason is,” said the Gryphon, “that they \Italic{would} go with
            the lobsters to the dance. So they got thrown out to sea. So they
            had to fall a long way. So they got their tails fast in their
            mouths. So they couldn’t get them out again. That’s all.”
        }

        \Para {
            “Thank you,” said Alice, “it’s very interest\-ing. I never knew so
            much about a whiting before.”
        }

        \Para {
            “I can tell you more than that, if you like,” said the Gryphon. “Do
            you know why it’s called a whiting?”
        }

        \Para {
            “I never thought about it,” said Alice. “Why?”
        }

        \Para {
            “\Italic{It does the boots and shoes,}” the Gryphon replied very
            solemnly.
        }

        \Para {
            Alice was thoroughly puzzled. “Does the boots and shoes!” she
            repeated in a wonder\-ing tone.
        }

        \Para {
            “Why, what are \Italic{your} shoes done with?” said the Gryphon. “I
            mean, what makes them so shiny?”
        }

        \Para {
            Alice looked down at them, and considered a little before she gave
            her answer. “They’re done with blacking, I believe.”
        }

        \Para {
            “Boots and shoes under the sea,” the Gry\-phon went on in a deep
            voice, “are done with whiting. Now you know.”
        }

        \Para {
            “And what are they made of?” Alice asked in a tone of great
            curiosity.
        }

        \Para {
            “Soles and eels, of course,” the Gryphon replied rather impatiently:
            “any shrimp could have told you that.”
        }

        \Para {
            “If I’d been the whiting,” said Alice, whose thoughts were still
            running on the song, “I’d have said to the porpoise, ‘Keep back,
            please: we don’t want \Italic{you} with us!’”
        }

        \Para {
            “They were obliged to have him with them,” the Mock Turtle said: “no
            wise fish would go anywhere without a porpoise.”
        }

        \Para {
            “Wouldn’t it really?” said Alice in a tone of great surprise.
        }

        \Para {
            “Of course not,” said the Mock Turtle: “why, if a fish came to
            \Italic{me,} and told me he was going a journey, I should say ‘With
            what porpoise?’”
        }

        \Para {
            “Don’t you mean ‘purpose?’” said Alice.
        }

        \Para {
            “I mean what I say,” the Mock Turtle replied in an offended tone.
            And the Gryphon added, “Come, let’s hear some of \Italic{your}
            adventures.”
        }

        \Para {
            “I could tell you my adventures—beginning from this morning,” said
            Alice a little timidly: “but it’s no use going back to yesterday,
            because I was a different person then.”
        }

        \Para {
            “Explain all that,” said the Mock Turtle.
        }

        \Para {
            “No, no! the adventures first,” said the Gryphon in an impatient
            tone: “explanations take such a dreadful time.”
        }

        \Para {
            So Alice began telling them her adventures from the time when she
            first saw the White Rabbit: she was a little nervous about it just
            at first, the two creatures got so close to her, one on each side,
            and opened their eyes and mouths so \Italic{very} wide, but she
            gained courage as she went on. Her listeners were perfectly quiet
            till she got to the part about her repeating “\Italic{You are old,
            Father William,}” to the Cater\-pillar, and the words all coming
            different, and then the Mock Turtle drew a long breath, and said,
            “That’s very curious.”
        }

        \Para {
            “It’s all about as curious as it can be,” said the Gryphon.
        }

        \Para {
            “It all came different!” the Mock Turtle repeated thoughtfully. “I
            should like to hear her try and repeat something now. Tell her to
            begin.” He looked at the Gryphon as if he thought it had some kind
            of authority over Alice.
        }

        \Para {
            “Stand up and repeat ‘\Italic{’Tis the voice of the sluggard,}’”
            said the Gryphon.
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Page 157 (Recto): Top-left Wrap #36 (Lobster) + 4 verse + 2 prose
        // ============================================================
        \WrapLeftStack(
            src: "images/tenniel_36.png",
            img_offset_x: -16pt,
            img_offset_y: 3.4pt,
            narrow_x: 144pt,
            min_grid_lines: 15.2,
            break_before: Break.Page
        ) {
            \Para {
                “How the crea\-tures order one about, and make one re\-peat
                les\-sons!” thought Alice, “I might just as well be at school at
                once.” How\-ever, she got up, and began to re\-peat it, but her head
                was so full of the Lob\-ster\=Quadrille, that she hardly knew what
                she was say\-ing, and the words came very queer indeed:—
            }
        }

        \PoemBlock(size: 10pt, leading: 16.0pt, italic: true) {
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 10pt, indent: 0pt) { “’Tis the voice of the lobster; I heard him declare, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 10pt, indent: 0pt) { ‘You have baked me too brown, I must sugar my hair.’ }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 10pt, indent: 0pt) { As a duck with its eyelids, so he with his nose }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 10pt, indent: 0pt) { Trims his belt and his buttons, and turns out his toes.” }
            \Spacer(height: 6pt)
        }

        \Para {
            “That’s different from what \Italic{I} used to say when I was a
            child,” said the Gryphon.
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Page 158 (Verso): Explaining the Lobster's toes
        // ============================================================
        \Para(pagination: \Pagination(breakable: true, break_before: Break.Page)) {
            “Well, I never heard it before,” said the Mock Turtle; “but it
            sounds uncommon nonsense.”
        }

        \Para {
            Alice said nothing: she had sat down again with her face in her
            hands, wondering if any\-thing would \Italic{ever} happen in a natural
            way again.
        }

        \Para {
            “I should like to have it explained,” said the Mock Turtle.
        }

        \Para {
            “She can’t explain it,” said the Gryphon hastily. “Go on with the
            next verse.”
        }

        \Para {
            “But about his toes?” the Mock Turtle persisted. “How \Italic{could}
            he turn them out with his nose, you know?”
        }

        \Para {
            “It’s the first position in dancing,” Alice said; but
            she was dreadfully puzzled by the whole thing, and longed to change the subject.
        }

        \Para {
            “Go on with the next verse,” the Gryphon repeated impatiently: “it
            begins ‘\Italic{I passed by his garden.}’”
        }

        \Para {
            Alice did not dare to disobey, though she felt sure it would all
            come wrong, and she went on in a trembling voice:—
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Page 159 (Recto): 1866 2-line Owl & Oyster verse + Turtle Soup intro
        // ============================================================
        \PoemBlock(size: 10pt, leading: 15.5pt, italic: true, break_before: Break.Page) {
            \Spacer(height: 4pt)
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 10pt, indent: 0pt) { “I passed by his garden, and marked, with one eye, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 10pt, indent: 0pt) { How the owl and the oyster were sharing a pie—” }
            \Spacer(height: 24pt)
        }

        \Para {
            “What \Italic{is} the use of repeating all that stuff,” the Mock
            Turtle interrupted, “if you don’t explain it as you go on? It’s by
            far the most confusing thing \Italic{I} ever heard!”
        }

        \Para {
            “Yes, I think you’d better leave off,” said the Gryphon, and Alice
            was only too glad to do so.
        }

        \Para {
            “Shall we try another figure of the Lobster-Quadrille?” the Gryphon
            went on. “Or would you like the Mock Turtle to sing you a song?”
        }

        \Para {
            “Oh, a song, please, if the Mock Turtle would be so kind,” Alice
            replied, so eagerly that the Gryphon said, in a rather offended
            tone, “Hm! No accounting for tastes! Sing her ‘\Italic{Turtle
            Soup,}’ will you, old fellow?”
        }

        \Para {
            The Mock Turtle sighed deeply, and began, in a voice sometimes
            choked with sobs, to sing this:—
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Pages 160–161: "Beautiful Soup" & "The trial's beginning!"
        // ============================================================
        \PoemBlock(size: 10pt, leading: 15.5pt, italic: true, break_before: Break.Page) {
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 36pt, indent: 0pt) { “Beautiful Soup, so rich and green, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 36pt, indent: 0pt) { Waiting in a hot tureen! }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 36pt, indent: 0pt) { Who for such dainties would not stoop? }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 36pt, indent: 0pt) { Soup of the evening, beautiful Soup! }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 36pt, indent: 0pt) { Soup of the evening, beautiful Soup! }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 36pt, indent: 20pt) { Beau—ootiful Soo—oop! }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 36pt, indent: 20pt) { Beau—ootiful Soo—oop! }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 36pt, indent: 0pt) { Soo—oop of the e—e—evening, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 36pt, indent: 20pt) { Beautiful, beautiful Soup! }

            \Spacer(height: 28pt)

            \VerseLine(left_margin: 36pt, indent: 0pt) { “Beautiful Soup! Who cares for fish, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 36pt, indent: 0pt) { Game, or any other dish? }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 36pt, indent: 0pt) { Who would not give all else for two p }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 36pt, indent: 0pt) { ennyworth only of beautiful Soup? }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 36pt, indent: 0pt) { Pennyworth only of beautiful Soup? }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 36pt, indent: 20pt) { Beau—ootiful Soo—oop! }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 36pt, indent: 20pt) { Beau—ootiful Soo—oop! }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 36pt, indent: 0pt) { Soo—oop of the e—e—evening, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 36pt, indent: 20pt) { Beautiful, beauti—FUL SOUP!” }
            \Spacer(height: 20pt)
        }

        \Para {
            “Chorus again!” cried the Gryphon, and the Mock Turtle had just
            begun to repeat it, when a cry of “The trial’s beginning!” was heard
            in the distance.
        }

        \Para {
            “Come on!” cried the Gryphon, and, taking Alice by the hand, it
            hurried off, without wait\-ing for the end of the song.
        }

        \Para {
            “What trial is it?” Alice panted as she ran, but the Gryphon only
            answered “Come on!” and ran the faster, while more and more faintly
            came, carried on the breeze that followed them, the melancholy
            words:—
        }

        \PoemBlock(size: 10pt, leading: 15.5pt, italic: true) {
            \Spacer(height: 10pt)
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 33pt, indent: 0pt) { “Soo—oop of the e—e—evening, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 54pt, indent: 0pt) { Beautiful, beautiful Soup!” }
        }

        \ChapterEnd()
    };

    \alias right: Distance = x + width;
    \alias height: Distance = ch.height;
    \alias bottom: Distance = y + height;
}
